      "level" : "Doctitle",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 53.098, 698.687, 162.202, 718.463 ],
      "heading level" : 1,
      "font" : "Galliard-UltraItalic",
      "font size" : 16.0,
      "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
      "content" : "Clinical Pearl"
    }, {
      "type" : "paragraph",
      "pdfua_tag" : "P",
      "id" : 3,
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 53.098, 604.793, 316.922, 697.469 ],
      "font" : "Galliard-Italic",
      "font size" : 11.0,
      "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
      "content" : "With Chlamydia conjunctivitis, find out if the patient has been exposed to domesticated birds, particularly parakeets. A sizeable number of U.S. parakeets carry the Chlamydia organism in their bodies as Chlamydia psittacosis, which causes a clinically identical picture to sexually transmitted disease. Treatment is the same: one 1000-mg dose of oral azithromycin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H2",
    "id" : 22,
    "level" : "Subtitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 345.839, 566.642, 442.787, 625.058 ],
    "heading level" : 2,
    "font" : "Galliard-Ultra",
    "font size" : 12.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "Adult Inclusion Conjunctivitis (Chlamydia): Management"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 18,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 191.339, 512.041, 332.339, 578.642 ],
    "font" : "Palatino-Roman",
    "font size" : 10.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "any. One may also see a very inflamed upper tarsal conjunctiva with papillary hypertrophy once the upper lid is everted."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 14,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 36.839, 484.241, 175.889, 578.642 ],
    "font" : "Palatino-Roman",
    "font size" : 10.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "ampicillin (various), amoxicillin, amoxicillin/clavulanate potassium (Augmentin, GlaxoSmithKline) or a second-generation cephalosporin such as cefuroxime (Ceftin, GlaxoSmithKline)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 23,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 345.839, 456.441, 482.619, 564.742 ],
    "font" : "Palatino-Roman",
    "font size" : 10.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "Adult inclusion conjunctivitis is the ocular manifestation of a systemic condition; it must be addressed with systemic medication. There is no need for topical therapy, other than artificial tears if desired."
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 6,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 9.296, 313.626, 257.552, 527.322 ],
    "alt_source" : "missing",
    "source" : "075cc4e94eb8bdd518b96fc27aaef00a64c8_page_11_pg1_images/imageFile6.png"
  }, {
    "type" : "image",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Figure",
    "id" : 5,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 257.066, 313.626, 505.352, 527.322 ],
    "alt_source" : "missing",
    "source" : "075cc4e94eb8bdd518b96fc27aaef00a64c8_page_11_pg1_images/imageFile7.png"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 19,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 191.339, 373.041, 328.539, 509.142 ],
    "font" : "Palatino-Roman",
    "font size" : 10.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "If Chlamydia is suspected, it is important to ask if the patient has changed sex partners or had a new sex partner in the last few months. The patient may also have noticed burning on urination, although as many as 50% of those with Chlamydia are asymptomatic."
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H2",
    "id" : 15,
    "level" : "Subtitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 36.839, 413.742, 133.787, 472.158 ],
    "heading level" : 2,
    "font" : "Galliard-Ultra",
    "font size" : 12.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "Adult Inclusion Conjunctivitis (Chlamydia): Diagnosis"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 24,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 345.839, 331.341, 485.039, 453.542 ],
    "font" : "Palatino-Roman",
    "font size" : 10.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "Key to successful management of Chlamydia conjunctivitis is concurrent treatment of both sexual partners so that they don’t re-infect one another. For compliance and efficacy, the ideal treatment is a single 1000-mg dose of oral azithromycin,"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 16,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 36.839, 234.041, 174.099, 411.842 ],
    "font" : "Palatino-Roman",
    "font size" : 10.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "Adult inclusion conjunctivitis, caused by Chlamydia, often manifests as a lingering, low-grade red eye with scant mucous discharge. The patient may have been selftreating for some time. Often, these cases are seen on referral from a general practitioner who treated the patient unsuccessfully with a topical eye drop that did not resolve the condition."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 20,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 191.339, 220.141, 325.899, 370.142 ],
    "font" : "Palatino-Roman",
    "font size" : 10.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "Many optometrists are uncomfortable asking such personal questions, but there is no need to be embarrassed about clinically relevant details. One approach is to tell the patient that you suspect he or she has a sexually transmitted disease, so the questions don’t seem out of the ordinary. Another"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 26,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 345.839, 289.641, 481.039, 342.342 ],
    "font" : "Palatino-Roman",
    "font size" : 10.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "although 3and 5-day courses of azithromycin have also been shown clinically to be effective."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 25,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 409.899, 336.06, 414.999, 341.561 ],
    "font" : "Palatino-Roman",
    "font size" : 5.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "34"
  }, {
